      <header class="page-header">
        <h1>Dead Hang Injury Prevention: Protect Your Shoulders, Elbows & Wrists</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">Dead hangs are low impact, but overuse injuries and falls happen. Most follow progressing too fast or ignoring pain signals. This guide covers common causes and ways to reduce the risk.</p>
      </header>
    </div>
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-meta">
        <a href="/about/" class="author-link">
          <span class="author-avatar">DH</span>
          <span>DeadHangs Team</span>
        </a>
        <span class="meta-sep">·</span>
        <span class="updated-date">Updated October 30, 2026</span>
        
      </div>
      <div class="container">

        <h2>Common Dead Hang Injuries</h2>
        <p>A dead hang loads the fingers, wrists, elbows and shoulders at the same time, and a problem tends to show up at the weakest point in that chain. Knowing the common problem areas helps you spot trouble early.</p>

        <h3>Shoulder Strain</h3>
        <p>The shoulder joint supports your full bodyweight in overhead flexion during a dead hang. Shoulder strain can follow hanging too long or too hard before the tissues have adapted. Tight shoulders, existing shoulder problems and cold muscles may raise the risk.</p>

        <h3>Elbow Tendinitis</h3>
        <p>The forearm tendons attach at the medial and lateral epicondyles of the elbow. Rapid increases in hang volume can overload these tendons. Medial epicondylitis (golfer's elbow) affects the flexor tendons. Lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow) affects the extensor tendons. Both present as elbow pain that worsens with gripping.</p>

        <h3>Wrist Pain</h3>
        <p>Some people feel wrist pain in an overhand grip. Pain may show up on the back of the wrist during or after hanging, and people with limited wrist extension or a prior wrist injury are likely to notice it first.</p>

        <h3>Callus Tears</h3>
        <p>Thick calluses can catch on the bar and tear during a hang. A torn callus exposes raw skin and usually heals in about a week or two. Training stops until the skin recovers, so prevention is simpler than treatment.</p>

        <h3>Falls</h3>
        <p>Grip failure during a hang drops you to the ground. The risk increases with fatigue, sweaty hands and bars mounted high. Most falls cause nothing worse than a jarring landing, but a fall from height onto a hard surface can injure the ankle, knee or wrist.</p>

        <h2>Shoulder Injury Prevention</h2>
        <p>A warm-up before a hang session is common practice, and it helps you feel ready. Evidence that a warm-up prevents shoulder injury in people who hang is limited.</p>
        <p>If a passive hang hurts, try a light active hang with a slight pull down. Active hangs ask the rotator cuff and scapular stabilizers to work, and some people find them more comfortable.</p>
        <p>Increase hold time and weekly volume slowly. A common guide is to add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly, roughly once a week. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Shoulder Protection Checklist</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Warm up with arm circles and band pull-aparts before each session</li>
            <li>Start with 5 to 10 second hangs in week one</li>
            <li>Add about 5 seconds to your target hold roughly once a week</li>
            <li>Stop immediately at sharp or catching pain</li>
            <li>Use partial bodyweight (feet on ground) if a full hang causes discomfort</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>Stop at any sharp, catching or electrical pain. Dull aching that resolves within minutes is common while adapting. Sharp pain is a signal to reduce the load and duration. The <a href="/shoulder-health/">shoulder health guide</a> covers shoulder conditions and when to avoid hanging.</p>

        <h2>Elbow and Tendinitis Prevention</h2>
        <p>Tendons adapt more slowly than muscle, over weeks to months. A sudden jump in volume can ask more of the tendons than they are ready for.</p>
        <p>Increase hold time and weekly volume slowly. A sudden jump from 3 sets to 6 sets doubles the load in one step.</p>
        <p>Avoid sudden jumps in added weight. Adding 10 kg to a 70 kg person increases the load by about 14%. Add weight in small steps and record what you used.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Forearm Exercises Coaches Use</h3>
          <p>These are wrist and finger strength exercises many coaches use for the forearm. They are not proven to prevent elbow pain.</p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Wrist curls:</strong> 3 sets of 15 to 20 reps with a light dumbbell. Works the flexors.</li>
            <li><strong>Reverse wrist curls:</strong> 3 sets of 15 to 20 reps. Works the extensors.</li>
            <li><strong>Rubber band extensions:</strong> 3 sets of 20 reps. Wrap a rubber band around all five fingers and spread them apart. Works the finger extensors.</li>
            <li><strong>Eccentric wrist curls:</strong> 3 sets of 10 slow negatives (3 to 4 seconds down).</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p>Many people do forearm work 2 to 3 times per week on non-hanging days. Forearm work may help; it is not guaranteed.</p>

        <h2>Wrist Pain Adjustments</h2>
        <p>Wrist pain during dead hangs can come from the grip or the hand position. Adjusting the bar or your hand placement sometimes helps without cutting training volume.</p>
        <p>A neutral (parallel) grip changes the wrist and shoulder angle and is worth trying if the overhand grip hurts. Parallel handles are found on many pull-up stations.</p>
        <p>Thicker bars are harder to hold, so cut your hold time if you try one. Fat grips or towel wraps add diameter to a standard bar.</p>
        <p>Some people use wrist wraps for support while the wrist adapts. Wrap firmly but not tightly around the joint, and treat wraps as a short-term aid.</p>
        <p>Reduce hang time and volume if wrist pain persists despite grip changes. If pain continues at low volume, see a physiotherapist for a wrist assessment.</p>

        <h2>Callus Management</h2>
        <p>Calluses form where the bar presses on the skin, and they protect against friction. Problems start when a callus grows thick enough to catch on the bar. A managed callus protects you, and a neglected one tears.</p>

        <h3>Filing</h3>
        <p>File calluses smooth 1 to 2 times per week with a pumice stone or callus razor after a shower, when the skin is soft. Remove the raised edges and leave the base intact. The goal is a flat, smooth callus rather than no callus.</p>

        <h3>Chalk Use</h3>
        <p>Gym chalk is magnesium carbonate. It dries the skin and increases friction between skin and bar. Use it if sweat, not fatigue, ends your hang. No study of chalk and dead hang time was found. Liquid chalk suits home training where dust is a concern.</p>

        <h3>Grip Rotation</h3>
        <p>Alternate between overhand and neutral grip across sessions. Each grip loads a different part of the palm, so rotating grips can spread wear across more skin.</p>

        <h3>Hand Care Routine</h3>
        <p>Moisturize your hands after training, because dry, cracked skin tears more easily. A non-greasy hand cream at night works well. Apply a climbing hand balm if skin splits develop between calluses.</p>

        <h2>Warm-Up Routine Before Dead Hangs</h2>
        <p>A warm-up helps you feel ready; evidence that it prevents injury is limited. This 2 to 3 minute routine prepares the shoulders, wrists and forearms for the first hang of a session.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/mobility/">mobility and warm-up guide</a> explains each drill.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Pre-Hang Warm-Up (2 to 3 Minutes)</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Arm circles:</strong> 15 forward, 15 backward, from small circles to large.</li>
            <li><strong>Wrist circles:</strong> 10 clockwise and 10 counter-clockwise per hand.</li>
            <li><strong>Band pull-aparts:</strong> 15 to 20 reps with a light band.</li>
            <li><strong>One or two short easy hangs:</strong> Stop well before your grip is tired.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <h2>When to Stop and Seek Help</h2>
        <p>Most dead hang discomfort is ordinary training stress. Certain symptoms mean you should stop training and see a medical professional.</p>

        <h3>Stop Immediately If:</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Sharp pain:</strong> Sudden, stabbing or catching pain during the hang is a signal to stop.</li>
          <li><strong>Numbness or tingling:</strong> Pins and needles in your fingers or hands during or after hanging. This can be nerve compression or restricted blood flow. Stop and get it checked if it returns.</li>
          <li><strong>Swelling:</strong> Visible swelling around the wrist, elbow or shoulder after training.</li>
          <li><strong>Pain that lingers:</strong> Joint or tendon pain that is still there on rest days. Ordinary delayed muscle soreness can last up to 72 hours.</li>
          <li><strong>Grip failure:</strong> Sudden loss of grip that does not recover between sessions. This may indicate nerve or muscle trouble.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Check with a clinician before returning after a diagnosed injury. If a warning sign appears, rest and see a physician or physiotherapist before resuming dead hang training. The <a href="/deadhang-safety/">dead hang safety guide</a> covers more precautions.</p>

        <h2>Returning After Injury</h2>
        <p>Check with a clinician before returning after a diagnosed injury. If you are cleared, one cautious option is to resume at about half your pre-injury hold time and rebuild gradually. This is common training practice, not a tested protocol.</p>
        <p>A neutral grip changes the wrist and elbow angle during the return phase. Choose the grip modification that avoids your specific injury site.</p>
        <p>Gymnastic rings rotate freely, which lets the wrists find a comfortable position. Some people with wrist or elbow problems find rings easier than a fixed bar.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Example Return Plan (Practical Guidance, Not Trial-Tested)</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Week 1:</strong> 50% of pre-injury duration. 2 to 3 sets. Every other day.</li>
            <li><strong>Week 2:</strong> 65% of pre-injury duration. 3 sets. Every other day.</li>
            <li><strong>Week 3:</strong> 80% of pre-injury duration. 3 to 4 sets. 3 sessions with a rest day between.</li>
            <li><strong>Week 4:</strong> Return to pre-injury training if pain free throughout weeks 1 to 3.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p>Any return of symptoms during the plan means the load rose too fast. Drop back one week and rebuild again.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Can dead hangs cause shoulder injury?</h3>
          <p>Dead hangs can strain the shoulder if you progress too fast or hang with a shoulder that is already irritated. Starting with partial bodyweight and building duration gradually lowers the chance of overload. See a clinician first if you have a diagnosed shoulder condition.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How do I prevent elbow pain from dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>Increase hold time and weekly volume slowly, and avoid sudden jumps in sets or added weight. Wrist curls and reverse wrist curls are exercises many coaches use for the forearm, but they are not proven to prevent elbow pain. Stop and see a clinician if elbow pain persists.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>When should I stop dead hanging and see a doctor?</h3>
          <p>Stop and seek evaluation for sharp or shooting pain, numbness or tingling in the hands, visible swelling, joint or tendon pain that is still there on rest days, or grip weakness that does not recover between sessions.</p>
        </div>

